  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the basic workflow of one-time pad cryptography. A readable message on the left is first combined with a one-time pad encryption key, shown as a key icon above the process. This converts the original document into ciphertext, represented by a page filled with binary digits and a lock. The encrypted data is then sent across a public transmission channel, emphasizing that the communication path itself does not need to be secret because the content is unreadable in encrypted form.\n\nOn the receiving side, the same ciphertext is shown again and is transformed back into the original readable message using a corresponding one-time pad decryption key. The layout highlights the symmetry of the process: plaintext becomes ciphertext during encryption, travels securely as encoded data, and is restored to plaintext during decryption.\n\nOverall, the image communicates that security depends on the one-time pad key rather than on hiding the channel. The central comparison is between the human-readable message and the binary ciphertext, with locks and keys visually reinforcing that access to the correct key is what enables successful encryption and decryption.",
